# BOHEMIA SIDE QUEST — PLAYABLE .bq (canon machine layer)
# S17 — THE SEED THAT DOES NOT COME BACK
# Shape: this winter against every winter. Faction wires: BLUES, CARAVANS.
# Grounded in real agronomy, no invention: hybrid seed yields hard once and its
# children do not breed true, so a farm that plants it has to buy it again every
# year forever; open-pollinated seed saved from your own crop yields less and is
# an inheritance. Worse, pollen does not respect a fence, so a hybrid field
# planted next to the saved line contaminates the saved line. Both sides are
# telling the truth: the trader really can get them through this winter, and the
# grower really is watching her only inheritance get bought out from under her.
# Systems AS the mechanic: caravans are the valley's only outside trade and a
# public humiliation of one is a real faction event. No proper names. CLOUT rides
# the deed: a small sack and an isolated field is #quiet, a signed block-wide
# plan is #notable, the full load on credit is #risky, telling his buyers in
# front of him that the sacks do not breed true is #reckless.

# ---- QUESTBOOK STUDY CITATIONS (QUEST STUDY LAW, 7/26/26) -------------------
# The corpus this quest is actually built from. Every id below resolves in
# records/BOHEMIA_QUESTBOOK_LAW_INDEX.json (3,672 citable findings mined out of
# the 152 studied quests in questbook/), and gates/quest_study_gate.js verifies
# the id AND the quoted law title verbatim. A citation is a claim the machine
# can check, never a name-drop.
# @STUDY Q071.P4  W3 (invisibly seeded, 100 hours early — our long-arc + fold seeding
#   applied: the cost is seeded now and only legible in ten years, when the line that fed her mother is gone.
# @STUDY Q030.W9  ATTRITION CASCADES
#   applied: a thin year is survivable; a thin year plus a bought line plus a debt is how a farm stops being a farm.
# @STUDY Q003.W1  THE DIRTY ERRAND
#   applied: the trader is honest about everything, which is what makes the credit the dangerous option.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@QUEST   bq_seed_no_return   The Seed That Does Not Come Back
@ACT     2
@FACTION BLUES
@ONCE    true

@ROLE grower  REQ  faction=BLUES     keeps_the_saved_seed=true
@ROLE trader  REQ  faction=CARAVANS  sells_the_sacks=true

@STAGE 10
  @LOG The farm at the edge is short a winter of food and a caravan is parked in the yard with sacks.
  @DO show_objective 10
@STAGE 20
  @LOG His seed makes twice the crop and its children make nothing. Hers makes less and makes more of itself forever.
  @DO show_objective 20
  @DO complete_objective 10
@STAGE 30 COMPLETE #quiet
  @LOG One small sack for this winter, planted on the far side of the ridge where the pollen can't reach her line. Nobody eats well and nobody loses anything permanent.
  @DO bond grower +18
  @DO faction BLUES +5
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 31 COMPLETE #notable
  @LOG Got the whole block to sign a thin year on purpose. All saved seed, smaller plates, and a line that still belongs to them in ten years. Everybody had to agree out loud, which is why it'll hold.
  @DO faction BLUES +12
  @DO set_flag signed_the_thin_year
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 32 COMPLETE #risky
  @LOG Took the full load on credit. Best harvest anyone has seen in nine years, a debt to people who travel, and a saved line the pollen got into. Next year we buy again. So does everyone.
  @DO faction CARAVANS +12
  @DO faction BLUES -6
  @DO set_flag owes_the_caravans
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 33 COMPLETE #reckless
  @LOG Told his buyers, standing in his own yard, that the second generation of what he sells is worthless. True, loud, and it cost him the season and me a road.
  @DO faction CARAVANS -15
  @DO faction_posture CARAVANS +1
  @DO set_flag burned_the_road
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 34 FAIL #quiet
  @LOG Nothing settled, and the planting window closed while everyone was still being reasonable at each other.
  @DO bond grower -12

@OBJ 10  "Hear both sacks out"        target=grower
@OBJ 20  "Decide what gets planted"   target=trader

# =====================================================================
# THE ASK. Theme in her first line. She is not against the sacks, she is
# against what the sacks make you next year.
# =====================================================================
@TALK yard  speaker=grower  entry=stage>=10,stage<20
  @SAY His sacks will feed this block through winter. I want to say that first because everything after it sounds like I'm pretending it isn't true. #tired
  @SAY What comes up from his seed doesn't breed. You save it, you plant its children, you get a field of nothing. So you buy again. Every year. Forever, and forever is a long word to sign.
  @SAY Mine came off my mother's rows. It yields less. It has never once asked me for anything.
  @OPT "Then keep yours and take the thin year."   [gate: none]  -> thin
  @OPT "People have to eat this winter."            [gate: none]  -> eat
  @OPT "Can you plant both?"                        [gate: none]  -> both
  @OPT (wait for her to say the rest)               [gate: none]  SILENCE -> both
  @NOVERB "Tell her the caravan will not come back next year"
@END

@TALK thin  speaker=grower
  @SAY A thin year on purpose. Smaller plates, every house, agreed out loud so nobody thinks they're the only one going short. #tired
  @SAY That only holds if it's public. A private sacrifice is a rumor by spring and a grudge by summer.
  @DO set_stage 20
@END

@TALK eat  speaker=grower
  @SAY I know. I've counted the block twice this month and I get the same number both times and it isn't enough. #dread
  @SAY So don't let me be noble at other people's expense. Go talk to him. Just don't come back and tell me his seed is a gift.
  @DO set_stage 20
@END

@TALK both  speaker=grower
  @SAY Pollen doesn't know about fences. Plant his field next to my line and by autumn my line is his line with a worse yield. #tired
  @SAY Far side of the ridge, wind off it, and it can be done. It's careful work and careful work is exactly what nobody has time for in a bad year.
  @DO set_stage 20  @DO learn pollen_crosses_fences
@END

# =====================================================================
# THE YARD. He is not a swindler. He is a man selling a real thing that
# really works, and the trap is that everything he says is accurate.
# =====================================================================
@TALK sacks  speaker=trader  entry=stage>=20
  @SAY Twice the yield. I have never once said otherwise and I have never once had to. #flat
  @SAY She'll tell you it doesn't breed true. She's right. I sell food, not heirlooms, and a family that starves this winter has no heirloom problem next winter.
  @SAY Small sack, full load on credit, or nothing, and I'm parked here until dark either way.
  @OPT "One small sack. Far side of the ridge."   [gate: none]  -> small
  @OPT "The full load. On credit."                 [gate: none]  -> credit
  @OPT "What happens if the block can't pay?"      [gate: none]  TRAP -> cannotpay
  @OPT "Your buyers should hear about the second generation."  [gate: none]  -> tell
  @NOVERB "Ask him where the seed is actually made"
@END

@TALK cannotpay  speaker=trader
  @SAY Then we come back in the spring and we're pleasant about it, and there's a conversation about the water rights or the yard or the road. #flat
  @SAY Nobody gets hurt. Things simply belong to different people afterward, and everybody agreed to it in a year when agreeing was easy.
  @OPT "Small sack. That's all."           [gate: none]  -> small   @DO learn credit_buys_the_yard
  @OPT "Full load anyway."                   [gate: none]  -> credit  @DO learn credit_buys_the_yard
  @OPT (walk back to the farmhouse)           [gate: none]  SILENCE -> thin
@END

@TALK small  speaker=trader
  @SAY One sack. Barely worth unhitching for, and it'll get you to spring with a hungry face. #flat
  @SAY Plant it away from her rows if you're going to be sentimental about her rows. That's honest advice and it costs me a customer next year.
  @OPT "One sack. Over the ridge."          [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 30
  @OPT (load the one sack and go)            [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 30
  @OPT "No. The block goes thin instead."     [gate: none]  -> plan
@END

@TALK credit  speaker=trader
  @SAY The full load. Nine years since anybody around here saw a harvest like the one you're about to have. #flat
  @SAY And next autumn you'll need me, and the autumn after that, and by then it won't feel like a decision anybody made. That isn't a threat. That's just how it goes and you can watch it happen from here.
  @OPT "The full load."                     [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT "No. One sack, over the ridge."       [gate: none]  -> small
@END

@TALK tell  speaker=trader
  @SAY In my own yard. In front of the people who were going to buy from me at the next stop. #dread
  @SAY Say it and it's true and it's finished for me here. Then think about who brings salt and medicine up this road next season, and whether they'll bother.
  @OPT "Say it loud enough for all of them." [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 33
  @OPT (turn to his buyers and say it)        [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 33
  @OPT "No. One sack, over the ridge."        [gate: none]  -> small
@END

@TALK plan  speaker=grower  entry=stage>=20,knows:pollen_crosses_fences
  @SAY A thin year, signed by every house, or a small sack over the ridge. Those are the only two that leave anything standing in ten years. #tired
  @SAY If it's the thin year, it happens in the open or not at all.
  @OPT "The whole block signs it."          [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 31
  @OPT "Nothing is signed. Let it lie."      [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 34
  @OPT (go back to the sacks)                [gate: none]  SILENCE -> small
  @NOVERB "Promise her the caravan will never come back"
@END
